piece of the brain to any other piece of
the brain your brain is an amazing

00:29:51.350 --> 00:29:55.520
information processing machine that has
to be able to transmit information very

00:29:55.520 --> 00:29:59.690
freely across across wide ranges of
space all right

00:29:59.690 --> 00:30:04.640
so we could build a network like this
which is where we have a lot of local

00:30:04.640 --> 00:30:08.510
connectivity however this is not
particularly good for information

00:30:08.510 --> 00:30:11.810
transmission right because if I had to
send information from the left side to

00:30:11.810 --> 00:30:16.730
the right side I'd have to go across
half of the circle okay not so efficient

00:30:16.730 --> 00:30:20.980
on the right hand side this is a
relatively efficient information

00:30:20.980 --> 00:30:24.500
transmission system because it can send
information from one side to the other

00:30:24.500 --> 00:30:28.880
very quickly however it loses the
capacity to have local information

00:30:28.880 --> 00:30:33.770
processing so there's no help by
neighboring regions to process

00:30:33.770 --> 00:30:38.150
information this middle piece right here
has the best of both worlds which is

00:30:38.150 --> 00:30:41.840
that you have local processing here as
well as these short these long-distance

00:30:41.840 --> 00:30:45.110
connections that allow you to transmit
information to the other side very very

00:30:45.110 --> 00:30:50.900
quickly so when we look at humans we
actually find that many that all of us

00:30:50.900 --> 00:30:55.460
have this sort of architecture and in
fact it's not just humans then we also

00:30:55.460 --> 00:31:00.140
see that across many different species
so we've studied humans we've studied

00:31:00.140 --> 00:31:05.120
macaque monkeys we have studied mice
Drosophila which is a fly and the

00:31:05.120 --> 00:31:09.830
elegans which is a nematode or worm and
what we found is that very very

00:31:09.830 --> 00:31:15.410
consistently there tend to be mostly
local paths but then these short these

00:31:15.410 --> 00:31:18.890
long-distance connections which allow
short cuts of information transmission

00:31:18.890 --> 00:31:23.390
across the entire brain and that's very
consistent across all the species but

00:31:23.390 --> 00:31:25.530
then if we just or that we've studied
this

00:31:25.530 --> 00:31:28.980
that we've studied I should say but then
if we just look inside of the human and

00:31:28.980 --> 00:31:33.450
we look at the variation in this feature
across humans we can actually see that

00:31:33.450 --> 00:31:38.040
that variation is related to the way
that brain works so this is a really

00:31:38.040 --> 00:31:42.660
beautiful study not from my lab but from
another lab that shows that this

00:31:42.660 --> 00:31:47.820
prevalence of short paths in the network
is actually positively related to full

00:31:47.820 --> 00:31:54.210
scale IQ so individuals who have higher
IQ tend to have relatively short paths

00:31:54.210 --> 00:31:57.960
through their brain networks and
individuals with lower IQ tend to have

00:31:57.960 --> 00:32:02.250
longer paths within their brain networks
and that's really interesting because it

00:32:02.250 --> 00:32:06.990
suggests that this network
representation of a human brain is not

00:32:06.990 --> 00:32:11.010
only an interesting correspondence to
social networks but it's relevant for

00:32:11.010 --> 00:32:17.280
understanding how the system works as an
information processing system okay now

00:32:17.280 --> 00:32:22.140
why do you think short paths would make
for better brains this so this is a

00:32:22.140 --> 00:32:25.050
simulation where we're just trying to
illustrate the fact that if you want to

00:32:25.050 --> 00:32:28.550
transmit information across this entire
system you have to have on average

00:32:28.550 --> 00:32:33.390
relatively short paths throughout the
entire thing but I also hope that this

00:32:33.390 --> 00:32:39.480
movie makes you start thinking about not
just where the paths are but how

00:32:39.480 --> 00:32:43.050
information gets transmitted and so I
want to make it I want to make an

00:32:43.050 --> 00:32:49.110
analogy to traffic and to roadway
systems because I think it's helpful to

00:32:49.110 --> 00:32:52.440
help us understand how the brain works
so on the left-hand side these

00:32:52.440 --> 00:32:56.040
structural wires that I told you about
earlier I think of them as sort of the

00:32:56.040 --> 00:33:04.290
highways of the brain but knowing where
the highways are is not as important at

00:33:04.290 --> 00:33:07.470
least to me living around Philadelphia
as knowing what the traffic is like on

00:33:07.470 --> 00:33:10.800
each of the highways right I really want
to know what the traffic is so that I

00:33:10.800 --> 00:33:15.540
can choose the correct path for myself
to get to Penn in time for my class all

00:33:15.540 --> 00:33:19.380
right so we want to make the just we
want to distinguish between the

00:33:19.380 --> 00:33:22.710
structural highways and the traffic that
exists on top of them now what I would

00:33:22.710 --> 00:33:26.970
really love to do and say to you tonight
is that we have a perfect measurement of

00:33:26.970 --> 00:33:31.110
traffic on human brains that's
non-invasive the answer is we actually

00:33:31.110 --> 00:33:36.150
don't have that however what we can do
is make an indirect inference of where

00:33:36.150 --> 00:33:40.690
the traffic is in
brain and the way that we do that is

00:33:40.690 --> 00:33:45.010
that we say well let's measure the
activity of every single brain region in

00:33:45.010 --> 00:33:48.429
this second and let's measure it again
at this second and let's measure it

00:33:48.429 --> 00:33:52.000
again at this second and every second
we'll take a picture of the pattern of

00:33:52.000 --> 00:33:55.360
activity across the entire brain and
then what we'll do is that will say if

00:33:55.360 --> 00:34:00.039
two regions of the brain are changing in
activity with one another then there

00:34:00.039 --> 00:34:04.120
possibly sharing information with each
other so in other words if two regions

00:34:04.120 --> 00:34:07.779
are increasing in activity together and
then decreasing in activity together and

00:34:07.779 --> 00:34:10.720
then increasing again together and
decreasing again together so they're

00:34:10.720 --> 00:34:14.230
following each other in their activity
we say that those two regions are

00:34:14.230 --> 00:34:18.879
functionally connected they're probably
responding to the same stimulus or

00:34:18.879 --> 00:34:23.770
possibly sharing information among them
so that's our measurement of traffic for

00:34:23.770 --> 00:34:28.599
a human brain and it's a non-invasive
measurement so then once we do that we

00:34:28.599 --> 00:34:32.500
can construct a second type of Network
for the human which is the traffic

00:34:32.500 --> 00:34:36.700
network we call it a functional Network
and an interesting question is how

00:34:36.700 --> 00:34:40.659
should I study that network to better
understand how cognitive processes

00:34:40.659 --> 00:34:46.810
function in a healthy human or in a non
healthy human and I actually want to go

00:34:46.810 --> 00:34:49.929
back to the illustration from social
networks because I think it actually

00:34:49.929 --> 00:34:53.950
helps us very much to understand what to
do here so here's an example of a social

00:34:53.950 --> 00:35:00.580
network that I want to draw on so this
is a picture of Caltech Facebook friends

00:35:00.580 --> 00:35:06.940
and so each dot here is a different
student at Caltech the color of the dot

00:35:06.940 --> 00:35:11.260
indicates the house that the student
lived in and so what you can generally

00:35:11.260 --> 00:35:16.060
see is a strong clustered structure or
we often call it community structure or

00:35:16.060 --> 00:35:21.460
modular structure and you often find
that over here most of the people who

00:35:21.460 --> 00:35:24.190
live in the yellow house tend to be
friends with other people in the yellow

00:35:24.190 --> 00:35:27.790
house that makes sense people in the
purple house here tend to also be

00:35:27.790 --> 00:35:31.359
friends with other people in the purple
house there are these really interesting

00:35:31.359 --> 00:35:35.500
people in the center that don't tend to
be friends with their own house but tend

00:35:35.500 --> 00:35:38.920
to be friends with at least one other
person in almost every house so those

00:35:38.920 --> 00:35:43.270
are really fascinating because they can
be important brokers of information

00:35:43.270 --> 00:35:46.540
between the different houses and that
actually becomes very important when we

00:35:46.540 --> 00:35:48.010
think about the brain as well because
there

00:35:48.010 --> 00:35:51.520
these areas in the brain as well that
sit between different clusters the other

00:35:51.520 --> 00:35:54.910
important feature I wanted to point out
over here is that there's a little red

00:35:54.910 --> 00:35:58.660
guy right inside of the purple house so
he's not friends with anyone in the red

00:35:58.660 --> 00:36:01.930
house but he's friends with everyone in
the purple house and we're pretty sure

00:36:01.930 --> 00:36:08.980
he should move so here is the picture of
what that sort of structure looks like

00:36:08.980 --> 00:36:15.430
in a human brain so here each of the the
circles is a region of the brain and the

00:36:15.430 --> 00:36:18.370
color code from the left hand side is
the same as the color code on the right

00:36:18.370 --> 00:36:22.540
so you can map across so for example
these regions over here are very

00:36:22.540 --> 00:36:25.810
strongly connected with one another
they're all located in the back of your

00:36:25.810 --> 00:36:30.160
brain which is the occipital cortex that
helps you with visual processing or

00:36:30.160 --> 00:36:34.900
processing information that comes in
through your eyes there are also regions

00:36:34.900 --> 00:36:40.630
of the brain over here in purple that
are important for audition so listening

00:36:40.630 --> 00:36:43.600
and also for a language and there are
regions of the brain that are important

00:36:43.600 --> 00:36:47.710
for many other functions as well what we
basically find is that there are these

00:36:47.710 --> 00:36:52.420
separate groups that seem to performing
perform specific kinds of functions and

00:36:52.420 --> 00:36:55.870
that's shown by their pattern of
activity they're dynamic pattern of

00:36:55.870 --> 00:37:02.170
activity and I like to liken this to an
orchestra where we have many different

00:37:02.170 --> 00:37:07.090
kinds of functions in an orchestra right
we have the brass we have the strings we

00:37:07.090 --> 00:37:11.410
have the woodwinds we have the
percussion and they tend frequently to

00:37:11.410 --> 00:37:17.430
be communicating with one another in the
sense that they're playing similar

00:37:17.760 --> 00:37:21.640
melodies specifically the strings but
that's also true for some of the other

00:37:21.640 --> 00:37:25.570
systems as well so we have that
separation and yet together it creates

00:37:25.570 --> 00:37:29.440
this resonant beautiful architecture
which is what we see in the human mind

00:37:29.440 --> 00:37:33.850
as well okay so this observation that
there are a really beautiful clustered

00:37:33.850 --> 00:37:37.600
structure inside of the brain and that
those clusters seem to code for specific

00:37:37.600 --> 00:37:42.460
kinds of functions is something that's
motivated many new questions across a

00:37:42.460 --> 00:37:46.180
wide variety of areas so I wanted to
specifically highlight these because it

00:37:46.180 --> 00:37:50.500
helps to showcase the interdisciplinary
nature of the investigations that are

00:37:50.500 --> 00:37:55.510
going on motivated by this work so
number one in psychology we ask how do

00:37:55.510 --> 00:37:59.710
these networked modules change in
different brain States number two in

00:37:59.710 --> 00:38:02.619
neuroscience what
rolls do neurophysiological processes

00:38:02.619 --> 00:38:08.799
play in the in these modules number
three in medicine how our networks

00:38:08.799 --> 00:38:13.180
altered in psychiatric disease or in
neurological disorders in mathematics we

00:38:13.180 --> 00:38:17.769
try to understand what graph models are
most like the brain and why in

00:38:17.769 --> 00:38:23.200
statistics we're trying to understand
how we can infer true signal from noise

00:38:23.200 --> 00:38:26.920
in the data in physics we're trying to
understand what role that network

00:38:26.920 --> 00:38:31.240
structure plays and material properties
that's actually extremely important for

00:38:31.240 --> 00:38:36.220
traumatic brain injury so if you have
this structural wiring in your brain

00:38:36.220 --> 00:38:42.279
right that's that's very heterogeneous
that actually is a conduit for force

00:38:42.279 --> 00:38:47.260
from a traumatic brain injury in a way
that differs across depending on where

00:38:47.260 --> 00:38:51.339
the individual was hit and so that's
this work is playing a very important

00:38:51.339 --> 00:38:56.769
role in understanding the effect of
traumatic brain injury on cognition and

00:38:56.769 --> 00:39:00.910
then in art what role does the network
structure play in the creative process

00:39:00.910 --> 00:39:05.589
and why so I think that all of these
questions are very exciting and I could

00:39:05.589 --> 00:39:10.569
certainly finish off the talk with these
questions but I actually want to place

00:39:10.569 --> 00:39:15.789
the statement out there that there's one
big elephant in the room from all of

00:39:15.789 --> 00:39:20.500
this work and that is that if I really
want to understand human cognition

00:39:20.500 --> 00:39:26.349
there is no single picture I could show
you that would explain human cognition

00:39:26.349 --> 00:39:31.329
and that's because a picture ignores the
dimension of time human cognition is

00:39:31.329 --> 00:39:35.109
dynamic it's changing constantly right
so if we want to understand how the

00:39:35.109 --> 00:39:39.160
human mind works we have to have an
approach that's dynamic in nature how

00:39:39.160 --> 00:39:44.140
are these networks changing as you think
about going home tonight or as you go to

00:39:44.140 --> 00:39:48.309
work tomorrow how did they change over
development how do they change with age

00:39:48.309 --> 00:39:52.990
how do they change when you're anxious
versus less anxious so this is really

00:39:52.990 --> 00:39:58.000
where the work needs to go so how can we
do that well one way is that we can take

00:39:58.000 --> 00:40:02.619
those same images of the brain and those
same patterns of activity that we

00:40:02.619 --> 00:40:07.740
acquire from magnetic resonance imaging
and we can say well let's extract the

00:40:07.740 --> 00:40:12.120
functional Network so that traffic
network just from the first two min

00:40:12.120 --> 00:40:17.400
of data okay and then let's look at the
next two minutes of data and construct a

00:40:17.400 --> 00:40:20.850
network of what paths were used there
and then the next two minutes of data

00:40:20.850 --> 00:40:25.380
and the next two minutes of data that
allows us to actually create an avi of

00:40:25.380 --> 00:40:29.430
the network reconfiguring over time
showing different powers of information

00:40:29.430 --> 00:40:33.600
processing and communication as a
function of time so where have we been

00:40:33.600 --> 00:40:37.170
using that we've been using that
technique to understand long term

00:40:37.170 --> 00:40:41.250
reconfigurations and human learning and
we've been focusing initially on motor

00:40:41.250 --> 00:40:45.420
skill learning and so that's relevant
for a child learning to ride a tricycle

00:40:45.420 --> 00:40:49.530
is relevant for anyone who plays a
musical musical instrument that's motor

00:40:49.530 --> 00:40:53.400
learning it's also relevant for anyone
who plays a sport that's also motor

00:40:53.400 --> 00:40:57.330
learning so how does this happen
how does motor learning happen in the

00:40:57.330 --> 00:41:00.990
brain and can we watch it happen in real
time and see how these networks are

00:41:00.990 --> 00:41:05.190
changing the way that we did that Alysha
lee in the lab is through a very highly

00:41:05.190 --> 00:41:11.550
stylized experiment which i've been told
is very similar to guitar hero for

00:41:11.550 --> 00:41:16.170
anybody who has played guitar hero
before so the idea is basically that

00:41:16.170 --> 00:41:20.310
human participants volunteers go into
the scanner and they see this pseudo

00:41:20.310 --> 00:41:25.200
musical staff here you can see there's
no clef therefore it's doing a musical

00:41:25.200 --> 00:41:29.790
and UK also are holding a response
button box in their hand that has four

00:41:29.790 --> 00:41:32.520
buttons
so this says press the purple button

00:41:32.520 --> 00:41:35.850
then the blue one then the green one
then the red one then the blue one then

00:41:35.850 --> 00:41:39.840
the purple one etc okay and then they
practice these passages over and over

00:41:39.840 --> 00:41:44.370
and over and over again over the course
of three days and to ensure that they're

00:41:44.370 --> 00:41:49.290
learning we calculate the movement time
which is the time from the first button

00:41:49.290 --> 00:41:52.860
press to the last button press and we
see how that changes as a function of

00:41:52.860 --> 00:41:56.250
time what you can see is that over the
three days of practice there's a

00:41:56.250 --> 00:41:59.640
significant decrease in the time it
takes them to play one of these little

00:41:59.640 --> 00:42:04.920
passages and that's similar to what any
of you would attest to if you play a

00:42:04.920 --> 00:42:10.920
musical instrument
or if you play a sport or if you learn

00:42:10.920 --> 00:42:14.940
to ride a tricycle when you were a child
okay so what we did in this experiment

00:42:14.940 --> 00:42:19.320
was to ask what is happening in the
brain as people are learning so here we

00:42:19.320 --> 00:42:23.940
know that behavior is changing we know
that output is changing says that what

00:42:23.940 --> 00:42:27.749
is happening inside that allows for
to change so what we did is exactly what

00:42:27.749 --> 00:42:31.440
I described before which is that we took
a two-minute window and extracted that

00:42:31.440 --> 00:42:35.940
pattern of functional relationships
between brain regions and then we did it

00:42:35.940 --> 00:42:39.690
again the next two minutes and again and
again we had 75 networks in a row for

00:42:39.690 --> 00:42:43.410
each person that mapped out that
reconfiguration of the brain over time

00:42:43.410 --> 00:42:48.329
and what we found is this beautiful
architecture which is that those strong

00:42:48.329 --> 00:42:52.680
modules that I showed you before are
still present but they tend to

00:42:52.680 --> 00:42:56.970
reconfigure at their boundaries so while
the modules themselves are relatively

00:42:56.970 --> 00:43:01.470
strong what happens at their
intersection changes a lot so you'll

00:43:01.470 --> 00:43:05.279
have regions of the brain that initially
are communicating with one module and

00:43:05.279 --> 00:43:08.490
then change over time so look at this
peach one is becoming more and more

00:43:08.490 --> 00:43:12.720
strongly connected to the yellow module
so it's switching over to its

00:43:12.720 --> 00:43:16.470
communication pattern to the yellow one
I like to think a little bit about dance

00:43:16.470 --> 00:43:20.730
partners and swapping dance partners in
this part of the talk because that's

00:43:20.730 --> 00:43:23.670
that's really what these regions are
doing is that initially they're

00:43:23.670 --> 00:43:29.039
partnering with one other region or set
of regions and then at some point in the

00:43:29.039 --> 00:43:32.400
dance they switch over and they partner
with a different set and that allows for

00:43:32.400 --> 00:43:37.529
a change in the pattern of communication
that is then supportive of learning new

00:43:37.529 --> 00:43:41.940
information so what we found over the
last couple years is that brains that

00:43:41.940 --> 00:43:46.769
are very flexible like this that's that
change these dance partners frequently

00:43:46.769 --> 00:43:51.930
throughout time are correlated with
individual differences in in how well

00:43:51.930 --> 00:43:55.890
that individual learns so the more
flexible the brain the better visual

00:43:55.890 --> 00:43:59.549
motor learning is the more cognitively
flexible that person is the higher

00:43:59.549 --> 00:44:04.319
working memory performance the person
has and better statistics in planning

00:44:04.319 --> 00:44:11.039
and reasoning so I think it's also you
know we can we can think a little bit

00:44:11.039 --> 00:44:14.369
about the orchestral example here too
because every now and then you have the

00:44:14.369 --> 00:44:18.599
flute not playing with other woodwinds
but playing with the strings instead

00:44:18.599 --> 00:44:24.960
right or every now and then you have the
trumpets playing with the bass or the

00:44:24.960 --> 00:44:28.739
harp and that's that change in
communication can really change the

00:44:28.739 --> 00:44:33.599
timbre of the music in the same way that
or in an analogous way to the way the

00:44:33.599 --> 00:44:37.079
brain is changing its pattern of
communication all right so that

00:44:37.079 --> 00:44:42.540
motivated me to ask the question
well if flexibility is related to these

00:44:42.540 --> 00:44:47.310
wonderful things like working memory and
learning and cognitive flexibility is

00:44:47.310 --> 00:44:53.760
there a way for us to change maybe our
lifestyle or something else to enable

00:44:53.760 --> 00:44:59.700
better more flexible brains and so what
we did is that we studied this wonderful

00:44:59.700 --> 00:45:04.290
data set that was actually acquired by a
fantastic professor at Stanford

00:45:04.290 --> 00:45:07.950
University his name is Russell pulled
rack and what he did is that he actually

00:45:07.950 --> 00:45:13.890
scanned himself twice a week for an
entire year on Tuesdays he was fasting I

00:45:13.890 --> 00:45:18.180
think and on Thursdays he had eaten
breakfast and then he also did an entire

00:45:18.180 --> 00:45:21.930
cognitive battery which is a set of
tests that test his cognitive function

00:45:21.930 --> 00:45:25.800
that day and filled out a bunch of
questionnaires so not only did he give

00:45:25.800 --> 00:45:30.240
us a year of his life doing this but
then he deposited all of that data

00:45:30.240 --> 00:45:35.610
online for any scientist anywhere in the
world to freely use and study so big

00:45:35.610 --> 00:45:39.240
kudos to restful track I think that's
really an amazing thing for a scientist

00:45:39.240 --> 00:45:43.290
to do and what we found studying his
data is that on the days when he was

00:45:43.290 --> 00:45:48.270
rested and fed he tended to have a more
positive attitude was also much more

00:45:48.270 --> 00:45:52.350
attentive and scored more highly on the
attention tests and he also had higher

00:45:52.350 --> 00:45:56.790
brain flexibility on those days so that
this is a correlative finding but it

00:45:56.790 --> 00:46:02.640
suggests a causal hypothesis we could
test which is that if we alter the

00:46:02.640 --> 00:46:07.380
amount of rest or food that someone has
in the morning that would change their

00:46:07.380 --> 00:46:11.160
attitude and that would also change
their brain flexibility now if I tell

00:46:11.160 --> 00:46:16.410
this story of this study to a elementary
school teacher they say oh well of

00:46:16.410 --> 00:46:21.570
course I know that when my kids are not
rested and fed they don't do very well

00:46:21.570 --> 00:46:26.010
but it's really nice to see some
empirical backing from neuroscience to

00:46:26.010 --> 00:46:31.440
support that now I also want to suggest
that there are some individuals who do

00:46:31.440 --> 00:46:36.270
